WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:04.100
Film Courage: Once you knew you were going
to be the showrunner for Home Improvement,

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:08.820
you did a lot of research, you’re prepared,
you show up, you are now facing this room

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:14.730
of writers most of whom you know...how are
you dealing with this and was it as you imagined?

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:18.070
Bruce Logan, Author/Former Showrunner/Screenwriter:
Well first of all, I gave them all homework.

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:24.170
It’s like kids in school today they have
to do reading over the summer, they had assignments.

00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:28.880
Their assignments were they had to come in
with maybe 4 to 6 stories a piece or 3 stories

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:29.880
a piece.

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:30.880
I don’t remember what.

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:34.610
But everybody had to come in with and deliver
something.

00:00:34.610 --> 00:00:38.540
And so already you have just a bunch of stuff
to work with.

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:44.340
I had stories that brought in and all of my
writers had stories, so we had a lot to talk

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:50.530
about and so they had stories and the other
thing that you talk about on day one really

00:00:50.530 --> 00:00:54.989
before you even get into the stories is the
arc of the season.

00:00:54.989 --> 00:00:59.719
What’s going to happen in the beginning
of Episode One of the season and what is going

00:00:59.719 --> 00:01:03.809
to happen in episode 22 or 24 however many
we were doing.

00:01:03.809 --> 00:01:10.790
And once you kind of set up the general arc
then you get to the stories because the arc

00:01:10.790 --> 00:01:14.400
tells you where you want the characters to
go.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:19.220
So the stories that these writers come in
with they are not that worked out because

00:01:19.220 --> 00:01:25.310
first you have to decide on your arc and where
you want your characters to go but I’m pretty

00:01:25.310 --> 00:01:31.160
confident that all of us came in with stories
that then actually did fit in with the arc

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:38.530
or we cold tweak to the arc and that’s how
we got started so ideally you get started

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:42.851
with just a burst of energy and everybody
is really excited to be there, it’s the

00:01:42.851 --> 00:01:48.030
first day of work, people are getting paid
to be writers in television in Hollywood and

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:49.170
it’s a privilege.

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:53.610
Film Courage: How do you know you are keeping
the story moving forward?

00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:57.079
Were there times when you thought you were
doing things that were great, jokes were funny

00:01:57.079 --> 00:02:01.780
but then you stood back and said “This story
isn’t moving forward?”

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:10.200
Bruce: In traditional, multi-camera sitcom,
there is always the tendency to make too many

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:15.170
jokes and slow down the scene because you
think you are being so funny with all of the

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:21.310
jokes people are telling but it becomes pretty
evident along the way if people are just standing

00:02:21.310 --> 00:02:24.280
around telling jokes and the story isn’t
being served.

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:31.590
Then if you have a staff of good people somebody
is going to say “Hey, don’t we need to

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:35.810
get to this story point sooner and could we
cut this?”

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:37.660
So that’s very common.

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:41.840
Film Courage: Speaking of cutting I’m thinking
of let’s say Three’s Company [80’s sitcom]

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:44.700
and sometimes the character Mr. Roper would
come in with just a look.

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:46.300
He didn’t have to say a word.

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:51.459
And the viewer already knew he was either
judging or Jack Tripper or somebody.

00:02:51.459 --> 00:02:54.430
How did you and the writers know let’s just
do this as a look.

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:55.790
Was this when the actors suggested it?

00:02:55.790 --> 00:03:03.959
Bruce: Well Tim Allen...when you work with
somebody the audience loves you get so many

00:03:03.959 --> 00:03:07.500
extra laughs with looks like that.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:17.840
As the show grows you realize a look on a
certain line you know is going to get a laugh.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:23.980
With Home Improvement the thing that made
that show a success Tim Allen was a very successful

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:29.950
comedian but the thing that made the show
a success was coming up with a worthy adversary

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:37.040
to play his wife (who was Patricia Richardson)
and we knew that if we wrote a scene and he

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:42.300
did something stupid and she gave him a look
the audience (especially the women) were going

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:43.300
to love it.

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:50.381
So you learn about those looks and what they
are going to give you comedically.

00:03:50.381 --> 00:03:56.900
In fact we did a whole show of Home Improvement
called ‘The Look.’

00:03:56.900 --> 00:04:03.989
What that was...one of the writers came in
with this idea and it was based on just the

00:04:03.989 --> 00:04:08.630
look that your wife gives you when you act
like an idiot.

00:04:08.630 --> 00:04:16.469
So this whole thing (the episode) was called
The Look and it featured a very funny comic

00:04:16.469 --> 00:04:22.580
named Blake Clark and he played the owner
of a hardware store on Home Improvement.

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:29.689
His wife played by a very funny ex-Groundling
named Shirley Prestia and he would talk about

00:04:29.689 --> 00:04:34.680
the look that he would get and I guess we
did stuff with Tim and his wife at home.

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:41.229
But with Blake and Shirley she would give
him one of those looks, she’d give him the

00:04:41.229 --> 00:04:45.840
look and the audience would go crazy and he’d
give the look back and Tim was in the scene

00:04:45.840 --> 00:04:51.409
too and he’d be looking at them giving each
other the look and the laughs they just spread.

00:04:51.409 --> 00:04:54.740
I mean no dialogue at all.

00:04:54.740 --> 00:05:00.659
Every time the director knew to cut from look
to look to look to look and the laughter just

00:05:00.659 --> 00:05:01.659
built like crazy.

00:05:01.659 --> 00:05:03.189
It was a really funny episode.

00:05:03.189 --> 00:05:08.270
Film Courage: Do you think this is what distinguishes
a lot of new writers versus more seasoned

00:05:08.270 --> 00:05:12.410
is knowing when to cut out some of the jokes
and to go to the looks?

00:05:12.410 --> 00:05:18.470
Bruce: You know it was what distinguishes
watching a show grow because you don’t know

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:27.119
when you start a show what is going to hit
from which actors so it’s learning the whole

00:05:27.119 --> 00:05:28.119
thing.

00:05:28.119 --> 00:05:30.500
It’s not just what’s on the page, it’s
what is on the stage.

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:39.099
So if the actors are really gifted physically
and you start to see audiences reacting or

00:05:39.099 --> 00:05:48.279
even in a single camera show you can tell
if somebody’s reaction is just hysterical

00:05:48.279 --> 00:05:54.119
and you make a mental note of that and then
you start to use that but it usually grows

00:05:54.119 --> 00:05:56.629
from seeing the actors work with the material.

00:05:56.629 --> 00:06:01.399
Film Courage: Forgive me but didn’t this
happen with a show you did called Duet where

00:06:01.399 --> 00:06:06.479
where two stars started out as main characters
and then it was flipped around?

00:06:06.479 --> 00:06:08.849
Bruce: That was a whole other bit of business.

00:06:08.849 --> 00:06:14.749
I mean that was...Duet was on when The Fox
Network just started and we followed Married

00:06:14.749 --> 00:06:18.810
With Children which was a huge hit and we
did okay.

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:21.909
Duet was a pretty funny show.

00:06:21.909 --> 00:06:29.470
The audience...the studio and network liked
the show but they felt that the two leads

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:34.560
weren’t as funny as these two side characters.

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:41.740
So it was one of these really weird situations
where the two side characters became the lead

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:47.179
characters of the show and then one of the
main characters stayed in the show but one

00:06:47.179 --> 00:06:48.240
didn’t.

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:52.759
It was bizarre, it was really bizarre.

00:06:52.759 --> 00:06:58.020
That was the first sitcom, the first acting
job for Ellen DeGeneres.

00:06:58.020 --> 00:07:03.569
She was in the spin-off of Duet called Open
House playing a secretary in a real estate

00:07:03.569 --> 00:07:04.569
office.

00:07:04.569 --> 00:07:05.569
Film Courage: I bet that was funny.

00:07:05.569 --> 00:07:10.469
Bruce: And the other person, it was between
Ellen DeGeneres and Paula Poundstone and they

00:07:10.469 --> 00:07:11.159
were both hilarious.

